---
import Case from '../../layouts/Case.astro';
import Figure from '../../components/Figure.astro';
import editor from '../../assets/img/eaton-editor.png';
import compare from '../../assets/img/eaton-compare.png';
import switchBand from '../../assets/img/eaton-switch.png';
import confirm from '../../assets/img/eaton-confirm.png';
import writing from '../../assets/img/eaton-writing.png';
import monitor from '../../assets/img/eaton-monitor.png';
import monitorLight from '../../assets/img/eaton-monitor-light.png';
import monitorDark from '../../assets/img/eaton-monitor-dark.png';
import monError from '../../assets/img/eaton-mon-error.png';
import monEmpty from '../../assets/img/eaton-mon-empty.png';
import monChannel from '../../assets/img/eaton-mon-channel.png';
import monNormal from '../../assets/img/eaton-mon-normal.png';
import monRecording from '../../assets/img/eaton-mon-recording.png';
import monExport from '../../assets/img/eaton-mon-export.png';

// The short case. The deck gives away the ending; the shipped editor has the first screen, shown
// whole once, and every later frame is a region of one of the Webflow page's own images cut to
// the thing its paragraph names: the comparison table under decision 1, the switch, the
// confirmation and the running write as three bands under decision 2, the monitor on the row and
// its light and dark drafts under decision 3. The canvas editor that lost the test isn't shown:
// only what launched can be. The monitor's other states fold into one details row above the
// close.
---
<Case
  title="Eaton — Kyle Xu"
  description="UX Design Intern at Eaton, June–August 2022. RapidLinkX, an IoT configuration tool for airport conveyor systems: a parameter editor and a fault monitor for airport engineers. In a comparative usability test the drag-and-drop editor was 35% faster and made 15% more errors, so the form shipped. 14 component variants added to Eaton's design system; in usability testing, operational efficiency +49.7% and error rates −36.8%."
  ogImage="/og-eaton.jpg"
  eyebrow="Eaton · UX Design Intern · June – August 2022"
  deck="Airport engineers set a drive's parameters and read its faults from one laptop. The faster of the two editors we tested, 35% quicker, made 15% more errors, so the slower one shipped."
  next={{ title: 'Heygen', to: '/projects/heygen' }}
>
  <Fragment slot="claim">A configuration tool for the drives that run an airport's conveyors.</Fragment>
  <Figure slot="hero" src={editor} name="The parameter editor" evidence="Shipped" loading="eager"
    alt="RapidLinkX's parameter editor: a blue header and toolbar with a Real-Time switch turned off, linked drives listed on the left with one selected, and a table of starred parameters with a value field, range, default and display checkbox on each row"
    caption="Real-Time off in the toolbar, starred parameters filtering the table, Compare at the right." />

  <section class="col sec snap" aria-label="In thirty seconds">
    <dl class="facts">
      <div>
        <dt class="meta">Scope</dt>
        <dd>RapidLinkX, an IoT configuration tool for the drives in airport conveyor systems, used by airport engineers: a parameter editor and a fault monitor over USB. I was the primary designer on the <span class="nb">day-to-day</span> product design; my mentor planned the work and carried communication across teams.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt class="meta">Constraints</dt>
        <dd>The hardware was designed in parallel, so we reviewed with its team every week or two and redrew what had moved. Regional sales, dealers, <span class="nb">in-house</span> engineering and an external development team all had a say. Pandemic restrictions kept us from the engineers themselves; secondary research and stakeholder workshops stood in.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt class="meta">Outcome</dt>
        <dd>In usability testing of the shipped tool, operational efficiency up 49.7% and error rates down 36.8%. The 35% and 15% in the deck are a different test: the two editors against each other.</dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
  </section>

  <section class="col sec chap" aria-labelledby="c-editor">
    <div class="head">
      <h2 id="c-editor"><span class="n">1</span>The slower editor</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="body">
      <p>In a comparative usability test, participants split between a <span class="nb">drag-and-drop</span> canvas and this form. The canvas was 35% quicker. It also made 15% more errors, and a wrong value on a baggage line stops the line. The form shipped, and the speed came back in smaller ways: starred parameters filter the table, and Compare sets the live values beside a saved configuration or the defaults. Under Eaton's NDA I can show only what launched, so the canvas isn't here.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="figs">
      <Figure narrow src={compare} name="Compare" evidence="Shipped"
        alt="The parameter comparison view: a breadcrumb reading Parameter Configuration, Parameter Comparison, the title Compare with default values, and a table with a current value beside the default on each row"
        caption="Live values in one column, the defaults in the next." />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="col sec chap" aria-labelledby="c-write">
    <div class="head">
      <h2 id="c-write"><span class="n">2</span>Writing to a drive</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="body">
      <p>An engineer on a routine round wants the tool linked and watching. One at a drive that has just failed wants nothing written until they say so.</p>
      <p>So <span class="nb">real-time</span> writing is a switch, off when the tool opens, and both routes pass through one confirmation that backs up the drive's configuration before anything is written. A running write says so and asks not to be unplugged.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="figs">
      <Figure narrow set="stack" caption="The switch, the one confirmation on both routes, and the write in progress."
        items={[
          { src: switchBand, name: 'The switch', evidence: 'Shipped', alt: 'The editor\'s header and toolbar: new, open, save, export and reset icons, then a Real-Time label with its switch turned off' },
          { src: confirm, name: 'The confirmation', evidence: 'Shipped', alt: 'A dialog, Confirm Write-in, over the parameter table: changes will be written in real time to the connected inverter, a ticked checkbox to back up the config of the connected device, a config name reading DC 01, and Cancel and Confirm buttons' },
          { src: writing, name: 'A running write', evidence: 'Shipped', alt: 'The top right of the editor with a toast over the header: Writing to device, please do not disconnect, with a Cancel button' },
        ]} />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="col sec chap" aria-labelledby="c-monitor">
    <div class="head">
      <h2 id="c-monitor"><span class="n">3</span>The monitor, in colour</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="body">
      <p>The same test set a <span class="nb">text-heavy</span> monitor against a <span class="nb">colour-coded</span> chart, and the chart won outright: participants found errors 40% faster. I drew it twice. The dark version read better; the light one matched the design system and shipped, its four channel colours adjusted until they held apart at a glance.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="figs">
      <Figure src={monitor} name="The monitor" evidence="Shipped"
        alt="RapidLinkX's monitor: the linked drive and recording settings on the left, eight channel cards across the top with the first four coloured blue, green, purple and orange and showing live values, a chart of the four traces over a hundred seconds, and a sixty-second overview strip beneath"
        caption="Four channels on one trace, a sixty-second overview beneath." />
      <Figure set="pair" caption="The two drafts: the dark one read better, the light one is the system's."
        items={[
          { src: monitorLight, name: 'Light', note: 'chosen', evidence: 'Prototype', alt: 'An earlier monitor in light colours: four stacked traces in blue, green, purple and orange over a white grid, the channel cards above' },
          { src: monitorDark, name: 'Dark', evidence: 'Concept', alt: 'The same monitor in dark colours: the four traces over a dark grid, a legend of the four channels floating at the right' },
        ]} />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="col sec chap" aria-labelledby="c-ship">
    <div class="head">
      <h2 id="c-ship">How it shipped</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="body">
      <p>The last six weeks were the <span class="nb">hand-off</span> to contractors new to Eaton's Brightlayer system: the flows went to them as annotated boards with a set protocol for questions, and what the system didn't have became 14 component variants added to it. In my final days I started a Bluetooth mobile version for work at height, where USB won't reach, and left prototypes and notes for whoever came next.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="col sec" aria-label="More states">
    <details class="more">
      <summary>
        <h2>More states</h2>
        <span class="meta">The monitor state by state: its error report, empty, setting a channel, live, recording, exporting.</span>
        <span class="link toggle"><span class="show">Show →</span><span class="hide">Hide ↑</span></span>
      </summary>
      <div class="figs">
        <Figure set="pair" caption="Six states of the same screen."
          items={[
            { src: monError, name: 'Error report', evidence: 'Shipped', alt: 'The monitor on its Error tab: the most recent entry, a fault with its error code, diagnostics and a suggestion, then two earlier alarm entries' },
            { src: monEmpty, name: 'Empty', evidence: 'Supporting state', alt: 'The monitor before any channel is set: eight empty channel cards, an empty grid and a cursor on the first card' },
            { src: monChannel, name: 'Setting a channel', evidence: 'Supporting state', alt: 'The monitor with a channel popover open: analog input 1 chosen, hide scope trace, auto-scaling, scale and offset steppers' },
            { src: monNormal, name: 'Normal view', evidence: 'Shipped', alt: 'The monitor with four channels live and their traces on the chart' },
            { src: monRecording, name: 'Recording', evidence: 'Shipped', alt: 'The monitor while recording: a blue band sweeps the chart, the button reads Stop Recording and a counter shows the samples' },
            { src: monExport, name: 'Export', evidence: 'Shipped', alt: 'The monitor with the Export Waveform dialog open: a name, an address, and PDF or CSV' },
          ]} />
      </div>
    </details>
  </section>

  <Fragment slot="close">
    <p class="stance">The slower editor was the right one to ship.</p>
    <p>35% faster is the number that sells a <span class="nb">drag-and-drop</span> editor. Its 15% more errors show up later, on a baggage line, not in a demo. The test put both numbers on one page, which is what it was for.</p>
  </Fragment>
</Case>
